	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44182</id><title>‘Safeguard the postwar order,’ China tells UN General Assembly</title><updated>2026-09-26T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44182" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/26-09-26_China_UNGA81.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/26-09-26_China_UNGA81.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/26-09-26_China_UNGA81.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/26-09-26_China_UNGA81.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;China&#039;s Vice President, Han Zheng, urged the world to defend the postwar international order on Saturday, telling the UN General Assembly that geopolitical conflicts, widening development gaps and mounting global challenges make multilateralism more urgent than ever.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44182&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘Safeguard the postwar order,’ China tells UN General Assembly”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44181</id><title>Countries adopt global blueprint to tackle fast-evolving crime in digital age</title><updated>2026-09-26T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44181" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/24-10-2022_Unsplash_cybersecurity.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/24-10-2022_Unsplash_cybersecurity.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/24-10-2022_Unsplash_cybersecurity.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/24-10-2022_Unsplash_cybersecurity.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Countries adopted on Saturday a new global blueprint for tackling rapidly evolving forms of crime, as the UN’s largest gathering on crime prevention and criminal justice opened in Abu Dhabi with a call for stronger international cooperation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44181&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Countries adopt global blueprint to tackle fast-evolving crime in digital age”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44180</id><title>The Takeaway: UN General Assembly debate Day 4</title><updated>2026-09-26T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44180" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/24-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-behind-scenes-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/24-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-behind-scenes-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/24-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-behind-scenes-03.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/09/24-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-behind-scenes-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;From the Caribbean to the Pacific, leaders from small island States demanded climate justice, ocean protection and greater solidarity at the UN General Assembly on Friday.  They warned that rising seas are already redrawing national boundaries, while the ripple effects of faraway wars are being felt on their shores. This edition of The Takeaway summarizes key points made during the fourth day of the annual high-level debate.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/26/44180&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The Takeaway: UN General Assembly debate Day 4”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44164</id><title>Nepal’s Devastating Floods Have Put Women and Girls at Greater Risk</title><updated>2026-09-25T16:04:11-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44164" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Ranjeeta-Silwal-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;
KATHMANDU, Nepal / NEW YORK, United States , September 25 (IPS)  - On August 26, 2026, deadly flash floods and landslides ripped through Himalayan towns and villages along Nepal and Tibet’s border, devastating communities. The United Nations estimates that in Nepal’s worst-affected districts of Rasuwa and Nuwakot, &lt;a href=&quot;https://unwomen.org.au/nearly-160000-women-and-girls-impacted-by-nepal-flash-floods/&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;157,050 women and girls are directly or indirectly impacted&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44164&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Nepal’s Devastating Floods Have Put Women and Girls at Greater Risk”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44161</id><title>Myanmar: Corporate Complicity in the Spotlight</title><updated>2026-09-25T14:41:47-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44161" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/A-Rohingya-refugee_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/A-Rohingya-refugee_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/A-Rohingya-refugee_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/A-Rohingya-refugee_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
LONDON, September 25 (IPS)  - On 15 September, Norwegian police and security services &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.ft.com/content/4b9a0db3-733b-4816-8931-de6bf49ffee6?syn-25a6b1a6=1&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;raided&lt;/a&gt; the headquarters of Telenor in Oslo. Authorities accuse the multinational telecoms company, majority owned by the Norwegian state, of aiding and abetting crimes against humanity in Myanmar and breaching sanctions.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44161&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Myanmar: Corporate Complicity in the Spotlight”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44160</id><title>Forty Years of the ‘Right to Development’: When Growth Excludes, Is It Still Development?</title><updated>2026-09-25T08:25:55-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44160" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/UN71153538_20260923_LJ_CR4-9_Low-Resolution.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/UN71153538_20260923_LJ_CR4-9_Low-Resolution.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/UN71153538_20260923_LJ_CR4-9_Low-Resolution-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/UN71153538_20260923_LJ_CR4-9_Low-Resolution.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS &amp; BULAWAYO, Zimbabwe, September 25 (IPS)  - World leaders have warned that the most vulnerable people have been left behind as the right to development has promised more than it has delivered, with development seemingly the preserve of the superrich and powerful.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44160&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Forty Years of the ‘Right to Development’: When Growth Excludes, Is It Still Development?”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44179</id><title>Haiti at ‘watershed moment’, Prime Minister tells UN, urging full backing for security force</title><updated>2026-09-25T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44179" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Haiti.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Haiti.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Haiti.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Haiti.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Haiti is at a “watershed moment” in its struggle against gang violence and needs sustained international support alongside stronger national security institutions, Prime Minister Alix Didier Fils-Aimé told the UN General Assembly on Friday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44179&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Haiti at ‘watershed moment’, Prime Minister tells UN, urging full backing for security force”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44178</id><title>Pakistan’s Sharif warns any diversion of Indus waters will be ‘treated as an act of war’</title><updated>2026-09-25T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44178" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Pakistan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Pakistan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Pakistan.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Pakistan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif told the UN General Assembly on Friday that any attempt by India to stop, impede or divert Pakistan’s share of the Indus River waters “will be treated as an act of war”. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44178&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Pakistan’s Sharif warns any diversion of Indus waters will be ‘treated as an act of war’”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44177</id><title>UN voices grave concern as fighting spreads across northern Ethiopia</title><updated>2026-09-25T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44177" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/28-12-2023-UNICEF-Ethiopia-UN0494082.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/28-12-2023-UNICEF-Ethiopia-UN0494082.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/28-12-2023-UNICEF-Ethiopia-UN0494082.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/28-12-2023-UNICEF-Ethiopia-UN0494082.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN said on Friday it remains gravely concerned by the continued deterioration of the security situation across northern Ethiopia, as fighting spreads across multiple fronts and increasingly disrupts humanitarian operations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44177&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “UN voices grave concern as fighting spreads across northern Ethiopia”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44176</id><title>Tuvalu urges decisive global action as rising seas threaten its future</title><updated>2026-09-25T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44176" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Tuvalu.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Tuvalu.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Tuvalu.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026-UN-Photo-UNGA81-Tuvalu.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Climate change remains the greatest existential threat to Tuvalu, Prime Minister Feleti Teo told the UN General Assembly on Friday, warning that the pace and scale of international action are still falling far short of what vulnerable island nations need.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/25/44176&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Tuvalu urges decisive global action as rising seas threaten its future”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0004s ch:1 -->